--TEST--
Core\Db::connect asks `db.connect` about the block's name before it reads any configuration, so an ungranted name learns nothing about which blocks exist — ADR 0067 §§ 2-3, ADR 0118 §§ 1, 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `db.connect` grants `main` and nothing else. Both names below are written
// into `nvs.toml` as blocks -- the grant is what differs, which is the point:
// a capability that is a list of blocks is only a grant for the blocks on it,
// and configuring a second one is not granting it.
try {
    var $db = Core\Db::connect("replica");
    echo "opened replica", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The granted name reaches the *block*, and this block is deliberately
// unopenable -- it names no `driver`. What that proves is the ordering: the
// refusal above is about the grant and this one is about the file, so the
// check that produced the first never got as far as reading the second.
try {
    var $granted = Core\Db::connect("main");
    echo "opened main", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// ADR 0118 § 5: a denial is the world refusing a call, and the program is
// still running underneath it.
echo "still running", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# Two blocks, one grant. `replica` exists as far as this file is concerned and
# is unreachable as far as the program is concerned.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
host = "127.0.0.1"

[db.replica]
driver = "postgres"
host = "127.0.0.1"
--EXPECT--
Core\Db::connect needs the capability `db.connect` for replica, which is not granted
Core\Db::connect: [db.main]: the block names no `driver`, so nothing says which database it is
still running
